# Deploying with no account: Reliant Forge

Before deploying, load the `reliant-forge-deploy` skill. It has the whole flow,
the limits and the exit codes. In short:

- Use it only when the project has no host yet and the user didn't name one.
  If you find `vercel.json`, `netlify.toml`, `fly.toml`, `wrangler.toml`,
  `firebase.json` or a CI workflow that deploys, deploy there instead.
- Good fits: a Vite app, a Next.js static export (`output: "export"`), a plain
  HTML folder with a Vite `package.json`, or a new Go API with Postgres made
  with `forge project new <name> --service <svc> --claimable`. Not a fit: a
  Next.js app that needs a server, an existing non-Go backend, ML or data work.
- When `forge` isn't on PATH, ask the user, then run it as
  `npx -y @reliantlabs/forge` (Node 18+).
- An existing site: run `forge project new --in-place --kind site --name <name>`,
  then `forge env deploy prod --claimable --plan-only`, then
  `forge env deploy prod --claimable --json`.
- Give the user the URL and the claim link. Tell them it goes offline 72 hours
  after the first deploy unless they open the link and sign up (free). Treat
  the link like a password and never post it publicly.
- An unclaimed backend has no outbound internet. LLM calls go only through the
  Reliant provider: run `forge skill load deploy/llm`, declare
  `llm = forge.LLM {runtime = forge.OnHosted {}}` and give the service
  `forge.llm_env()`. On an unclaimed deploy those variables stay blank until
  the platform enables the in-cluster gateway route, so make a missing key
  degrade the feature, never crash the app, and don't promise the user LLM
  calls before the deploy is claimed. Never call api.openai.com,
  api.anthropic.com or another model API directly from an unclaimed deploy.
- Don't use `--claimable` if the user is signed in (`forge login`, or
  `$FORGE_CREDENTIAL_HELPER` is set). Don't retry a throttled deploy in a loop.
